================================================================
(a) proposed spec text
================================================================

prompts as context entities (decided 2026-08-12)
--------------------------------------------------

We add prompts as context entities in a Isabelle theory. This allows
Isabelle user to write a <mcp_prompt> command in their theories and the
associated text will get served to the agent. We believe that the
best prompts and guidance can be given by the authors of a theory
and therefore having prompts encoded into Isabelle theories is best.

As an example a very general prompt could be

<mcp_prompt> name =
<text>
Step by step description of the workflow.
</text>

Then the user can reference the prompt with /name in their coding
agent of choice. 

the registry: a third MCP_Registry instance
- - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - -

MCP_Prompt is an exact mirror of MCP_Tool and MCP_Resource by
construction — the same functor, so positions, ctrl+click,
completion, theory-qualified names, import-following visibility,
merge-by-union, and the activation set all arrive for free:

    structure Registry =
      MCP_Registry(
        val kind = "mcp_prompt"
        val changed = "MCP.prompts_changed"
        type value = prompt);

with the matching `mcp_prompts add:/del:` declaration attribute
(PLURAL, same lexer reason as the other two) and `print_mcp_prompts`
("!" includes inactive). bundles therefore scope prompts with zero
new machinery, exactly as for tools: a bundle that activates a family
of tools brings its prompts along.

the prompt value type
- - - - - - - - - - - -

a tool returns a string; a resource returns a string; a prompt
returns a MESSAGE ARRAY. that is the one place the mirror stops being
mechanical:

    datatype role = User | Assistant
    datatype content = Text of string          (*wave 1: text only*)
    type message = {role: role, content: content}
    type argument = {name: string, description: string, required: bool}

    type prompt =
      {description: string,
       arguments: argument list,
       build: Proof.context -> (string * string) list -> message list}

`build` is context-relative like `run`/`read`, so a prompt can quote
live prover state (the current goal, a theory's fact names) rather
than only static text.

ASSUMPTION the text of the prompt is resolved at prompt invocation
time and not at prompt definition time. The context of the invocation
is not the context of the definition and therefore a proof goal,
or in general the state of the prover is different between the two.

DECISION — arguments are FLAT, not MCP_Tool.param. MCP's prompt
argument descriptor is exactly {name, description, required}: there
is no type field on the wire. reusing the nine-constructor `ptyp`
universe would carry machinery that cannot be published, and would
re-incur encode_ptyp's tag-order hazard (a mis-ordered scala list
decodes Nat as Int SILENTLY) for zero client-visible gain. the cost
is a second small parser clause — `(arguments ...)` rather than
`(params ...)` — which is accepted. consequence to accept with it:
enum-valued arguments are unavailable, and the mechanism that WOULD
serve them (completion/complete over a ref/prompt) is already listed
out of scope for this phase.

scoping: the tool treatment, not the resource treatment
- - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - -

the two existing primitives are scoped DIFFERENTLY, and this matters:
`MCP.tools` takes (designation, bundles) and the designation picks the
context that DEFINES the tool set; `MCP.resources` takes designation
only (mcp_session.scala:658) and the resource scope_* family filters a
LISTING by theory glob.

DECISION — prompts take the tool treatment: `MCP.prompts` takes
(designation, bundles_yxml), resolves through
MCP_Protocol.designated_context_safe, and degrades to the empty
payload on a stale designation rather than leaving the client's
prompts/list unanswered.

DECISION — prompts ride the SAME designation as tools. no
prompt_scope_* builtin family is added; tool_scope_set /
tool_scope_include govern prompts too, and tool_scope_show reports
them. rationale: the designation is a standpoint, not a per-primitive
setting, and a bundle that activates tools for a task should not need
a second, separately-set scope to bring its prompts. the wart is the
name (tool_scope_* now governs two primitives); renaming is rejected
as a client-visible break for cosmetics. the change this DOES make is
client-visible and should be owned: tool_scope_show's reply grows a
prompts section, so a shipped builtin's output changes. additive, not
a break, but not invisible either. see review item 3.

the wire
- - - - -

    MCP.prompts (designation, bundles_yxml)
      -> MCP.prompts_result : list encode_prompt

a FLAT list, deliberately not the (rows, builtins) pair `tools_body`
uses. there are no builtin prompts — Isabelle/Scala publishes none —
so there is no mirror set, no drift gate, and no availability floor to
protect. the empty-payload floor is the empty list.

    encode_prompt =
      pair string (pair string (list encode_argument))
        (full name, (description, arguments))
    encode_argument =
      pair string (pair string bool) (name, (description, required))

    MCP.get_prompt (designation, bundles_yxml, name, args_yxml)
      -> pair string (pair string (list encode_message))
         (status, (error_text, messages))

status is "ok" | "error", mirroring run_tool / read_resource, and the
inactive policy mirrors them too: a registered-but-inactive prompt is
not gettable, matching its absence from the listing. the shape is
FIXED in both branches (on "ok" error_text is ""; on "error" messages
is []) — the same discipline as empty_tools_body still being a pair.

    encode_message = pair string string (role_tag, text)

role_tag is "user" | "assistant". a bare pair rather than a variant
while content is text-only; widening to image / resource_link /
embedded resource turns the second component into a variant, and THAT
is where the tag-order hazard would apply — noted now so the widening
wave does not rediscover it.

Isabelle/Scala
- - - - - - - -

- `initialize` gains `"prompts" -> JSON.Object("listChanged" -> true)`
  (mcp_server.scala:1303).
- `prompts/list` and `prompts/get` cases join the resources cases
  (mcp_server.scala:1421–1441).
- the readiness() split applies as it does to tools/list, but with the
  opposite floor: Not_Ready / Failed serve an EMPTY prompts list, not
  a static table, because no prompt exists outside the prover.
- error mapping: unknown prompt name and missing required argument are
  JSON-RPC -32602 (invalid params), NOT an isError content result.
  prompts have no isError channel — that is a tools/call-only affordance.
- name exposure: reuse MCP_Server.exposure() over the prompt row set,
  with its OWN reserved set (empty — no builtins to shadow). MCP's
  prompt schema does not itself impose the tool-name regex the
  sanitizer was written for, but theory-qualified names carry dots and
  clients surface prompts as slash commands, so the same
  base-name-when-unambiguous / sanitized-qualified-name-on-collision
  treatment is wanted for the same practical reason.
- listChanged: MCP_Registry.notify_changed already emits
  MCP.prompts_changed for free; Scala adds a prompts_changed handler
  beside tools_changed / resources_changed (mcp_session.scala:560–573).
  the server pins protocol version 2025-03-26 (mcp_server.scala:19),
  where the notification is the bare `notifications/prompts/list_changed`
  — NOT the subscriptions/listen flow of the 2026-07-28 draft spec.

the isar surface
- - - - - - - - -

header: `mcp_prompt` :: thy_decl and `print_mcp_prompts` :: diag are
new keywords. `arguments`, `messages`, `user`, `assistant` are matched
by CONTENT with Args.$$$, the trick already used for `optional` / `enum`
/ `list` / `of`. declaring `user` or `assistant` as minor keywords would
lex them in every IMPORTING theory, breaking unrelated uses of two very
common words.

    mcp_prompt prove_lemma
      (description ‹set up a proof of a named lemma in a fresh repl›)
      (arguments
        lemma_name ‹the lemma to prove›
        style (optional) ‹"isar" or "apply"›)
      (messages
        user ‹Prove $lemma_name in the $style style. Start by calling
              repl_init, then work in small repl_step increments.›)

argument interpolation reuses HALF of the tools' machinery, and the
split matters (checked 2026-08-12 against MCP_Combinators):

- REUSED: `scan_format` / `placeholders` / `check_format` — the
  `$name` grammar itself and the registration-time gates (unknown
  `$name` in the text, required argument never used). free.
- NOT REUSED: `assemble`. it is TYPE-DIRECTED QUOTING, built to make
  values lex correctly as Isar command arguments — String becomes an
  inner-string quoted+escaped, Source/Term/Typ get cartouche-framed.
  applied to prose a human reads, it would splice quotes and
  cartouches into the message text. prompts need a plain substituter
  (~5 lines over the same chunk list).

so "no second templating language" holds — same `$name` surface, same
error messages — but the substitution FUNCTION is separate. worth
being exact about, because reusing `assemble` verbatim is the obvious
wrong move.

`(optional)` is NOT literally the params clause's `optional_flag`
either: there it sits after the TYPE (`x :: string (optional)`), and
flat arguments have no type, so the flag necessarily moves to follow
the name. same spelling and meaning, a second one-line parser.

argument ORDER IS SIGNIFICANT — see the client note below.

the ML hatch, for a prompt that must compute its messages:

    mcp_prompt explain_state = ‹fn ctxt => fn args => [...]›
      (description ‹...›) (arguments ...)

the `messages` clause and the `= ‹ML›` form are mutually exclusive,
the same conflict check mcp_resource already makes between its
`(isar ...)` clause and its ML form.

the client actually renders these (VERIFIED 2026-08-12)
- - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - -

the rendering-app section of this spec records the lesson the hard
way: a client-side mechanism must be VERIFIED before the design is
worth writing. checked against the claude code MCP docs, 2026-08-12:

  "MCP servers can expose prompts that become available as commands
  in Claude Code. […] MCP prompts appear with the format
  /mcp__servername__promptname"

  /mcp__github__pr_review 456
  /mcp__jira__create_issue "Bug in login flow" high

three consequences the design must absorb:

1. ARGUMENTS ARE POSITIONAL, space-separated, in DECLARATION ORDER —
   not named. so the order of the `(arguments ...)` clause is part of
   the published interface, and a value containing spaces is quoted
   by the USER at the slash command. this strengthens the flat-
   argument decision: there is no named-argument channel for a type
   to discriminate on anyway.
2. the client namespaces the name as `mcp__<server>__<prompt>` and
   replaces every character outside [A-Za-z0-9_-] with "_" — the same
   mangling the tool-name sanitizer performs. a theory-qualified name
   (My_Thy.prove_lemma) would arrive as `My_Thy_prove_lemma`, mangled
   by the client rather than by us, unpredictably. that is the
   concrete reason to run exposure() over prompt names too.
3. list_changed is honored: "Claude Code supports MCP list_changed
   notifications […] tools, prompts, and resources […] without
   requiring you to disconnect and reconnect." the free
   MCP.prompts_changed emit is therefore not decorative — a prompt
   declared mid-session via repl_step reaches the menu.

wave 1 boundary (explicitly out of scope)
- - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - -

- image / audio content blocks
- resource_link and embedded-resource content (the natural future
