- builtin (scala-side) prompts — the whole surface is ML-registered

testing (the four layers)
- - - - - - - - - - - - - -

1. ml unit (MCP-Tools-Tests): declare/activate/deactivate round trip;
   merge across two importing theories; arguments encode/decode;
   missing required argument errors; inactive prompt is not gettable;
   `$name` interpolation including an absent optional argument.
2. scala unit (Fake_Backend): decode_prompts over a synthetic body;
   exposure over colliding prompt names; -32602 mapping for unknown
   name and missing argument; empty list served when Not_Ready.
3. bridge: MCP.prompts against a real designation; a bundle open/close
   changes the served prompt set; prompts_changed fires on a repl_step
   that declares a prompt.
4. e2e (test_mcp.py): initialize advertises the prompts capability;
   prompts/list is non-empty; prompts/get returns a well-formed
   messages array; a del'd prompt leaves the listing AND is refused
   (unlike a del'd builtin tool — prompts have no asymmetric
   callability, since there is no second substrate).

acceptance criteria
- - - - - - - - - - -

a user, writing only isar, declares a prompt with arguments in a
theory; it appears in claude code's `/` menu as
`/mcp__isabelle__<name>` and running it with positional arguments
seeds the conversation with the rendered messages; `declare [[mcp_prompts del: ...]]` removes it from the menu
and makes prompts/get refuse it; a bundle scopes it exactly while
open; ctrl+click on the name jumps to the declaration; the agent can
declare a prompt via repl_step and see it served after
tool_scope_set {repl: R} with no rebuild.


================================================================
(b) draft CHANGELOG entry
================================================================

2026-08-12 — mcp prompts: the third primitive, on the tool registry

  added "prompts as context entities" (phase 3)

  The MCP server exposes prompts that the user writes in theories.
  The underlying mechanisms is to add a Theory_Data just like
  in the rest of the MCP_Registry instances. The mechanism
  works as we expect and introduces a namespace for prompts
  that respects the activation and scoping.

  We restrict prompts arguments to be instances like {name, description, required}
  rather than user-definable typed arguments.

  the existing tool_scope_* designation rather than growing their own
  family; and the Not_Ready floor is the EMPTY list, since unlike
  tools there is no scala-side builtin table to fall back on. wave 1
  is text content only. the client half is VERIFIED, not assumed:
  claude code renders MCP prompts as /mcp__server__prompt with
  positional arguments and honors prompts list_changed (checked
  2026-08-12). no implementation yet — plans/mcp_prompt.


================================================================
(c) review items — decide these before the spec commit
================================================================

1. FLAT ARGUMENTS (highest-impact). the draft rejects reusing
   MCP_Tool.param. this single choice fixes the isar surface, the
   encoder, and the scala decoder together, and it is the one that
   most changes shape if reversed. the case against the draft: enum
   arguments are genuinely useful for a slash-command UI, and we
   already have the parser for them. the case for: nothing enum-typed
   can cross the prompts wire, so the type would exist only to drive
   an ML-side check on a value a human just typed into a form.

2. ONE SCOPE OR TWO. the draft has prompts ride tool_scope_*. the
   alternative is a prompt_scope_* family (consistent naming, but a
   third scope for the user to keep in sync) or renaming the tool
   family to scope_* (client-visible break). picking "ride" now is
   cheap to reverse before implementation and expensive after.

3. get_prompt's FIXED PAIR vs a variant. (status, (error_text,
   messages)) always encodes both halves. a variant would be tighter
   but re-incurs the tag-order discipline for a two-case sum. the
   draft prefers the fixed shape for consistency with
   empty_tools_body's stated rationale.

4. IS THE ML HATCH WAVE 1? the `= ‹fn ctxt => fn args => ...›` form
   doubles the command's parser work. dropping it makes wave 1 a pure
   declarative surface; keeping it matches what mcp_tool and
   mcp_resource both do, and "explain the current proof state" — the
   most obviously useful prompt for this server — needs it.

5. ARGUMENT ORDER AS INTERFACE. because the client passes arguments
   positionally, reordering an `(arguments ...)` clause silently
   changes the meaning of every existing invocation. should the spec
   say so as a stability rule (append-only after publication), or is
   that over-engineering for a wave-1 feature?

6. SHIPPABLE PROMPT SET. the spec section argues the primitive but
   names no concrete prompt to ship. a wave-1 set of two or three
   (prove_lemma, explain_state, port_apply_script?) should be decided
   with it, or the feature lands with an empty menu.
